id: pm-2qvq7a
title: "GH-1030: contracts --json --full is wholly omitted by the default output budget"
description: "The explicit complete-contract request pm contracts --json --full deterministically returns only a well-formed output-budget receipt under the default budget. Because stdout remains valid JSON and the actual contract is wholly omitted, downstream consumers can silently interpret missing command/action arrays as empty and publish structurally valid but content-free artifacts."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 0
tags[8]: agent-ux,contracts,full-projection,github-1030,json,output-budget,sdk-contract,silent-data-loss
created_at: "2026-08-15T10:41:12.886Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-15T16:32:07.639Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-15T16:20:44.949Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-15T16:20:44.949Z"
author: "harness:codex"
estimated_minutes: 300
acceptance_criteria: "Reproduce the exact contracts failure in SDK and CLI tests; define an SDK-native precedence rule where explicit complete-result intent defeats an implicit default ceiling while explicit caller budgets still win; make any unavoidable omitted_result=true outcome parser-visible through deterministic nonzero exit and transport semantics; apply the class-wide rule to CLI, SDK, and MCP; add negative controls for bounded partial degradation versus whole-result omission; manually verify packed npm, npx, Bun, and bunx consumers in temporary directories; document recovery and compatibility."
goal: project management = context management
objective: Make explicit whole-result reads semantically complete and make whole-result omission impossible to confuse with successful empty data.
value: Contract generators and agents can trust that --full means complete without carrying command-specific budget folklore.
impact: Downstream docs and integrations can silently regenerate zero-command artifacts while JSON parsing and process health remain green.
outcome: Whole-result intent precedence and omitted_result failure semantics are enforced once in the SDK read-output layer and regression-gated across every complete-result surface.
why_now: The regression is present in the current immutable daily release and has already produced an externally visible zero-command documentation artifact according to GitHub #1030.
parent: pm-5t33or
risk: critical
confidence: high
severity: critical
environment: Reproduced on repository main 996313a99bf7df942111cac40b8ada53107dcfe5 and pm-cli 2026.8.15; Linux; default output budget
repro_steps: Run pm contracts --json --full and inspect top-level keys. Observe only output_budget_exceeded and read_output with omitted_result=true. Run pm --output-budget unbounded contracts --json --full and observe the complete contract payload.
resolution: "Fixed by centralizing explicit-complete budget precedence and omitted-result failure projection in shared SDK/output primitives, with CLI, SDK, and MCP parity."
expected_result: "An explicit --full whole-surface read returns the complete contract by default, or fails in a parser-visible way before a consumer can mistake a well-formed receipt for an empty contract. CLI, SDK, and MCP expose one precedence rule and one failure contract."
actual_result: "The default invocation returns a successful, parseable JSON receipt with the entire result omitted. Live repository reproduction returned two receipt keys; the unbounded recovery returned 26 top-level keys, 131 commands, and 104 actions."
